(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-clinical-reasoning-documentation-quiz"]={"title":"Clinical Reasoning & Documentation Quiz — Physical Diagnosis 2 Exam 1","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What distinguishes a focused encounter from a comprehensive one?","choices":["The history and examination are both narrowed to what the presenting problem requires","The physical examination is narrowed while the history is still taken in full","The history is narrowed but every system is still examined","The encounter is identical but documented more briefly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a focused encounter is not a shorter version of everything, it is a targeted selection. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 7"},{"q":"How should subjective and objective information be arranged in a note?","choices":["Each kept in its own appropriate section, not mixed together","Combined into a single narrative so the encounter reads chronologically","Subjective information may be omitted when the examination is complete","Objective findings recorded within the assessment section"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — blending them is one of the commonest documentation errors. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 20"},{"q":"What should the opening statement of an oral case presentation include?","choices":["The laboratory and imaging results obtained so far","The past medical history and the chief complaint","The diagnosis and the proposed treatment plan","The complete review of systems, system by system"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — those two orient the listener before any detail follows. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 9"},{"q":"What is the test of whether an oral case presentation was a good one?","choices":["It lasts less than two minutes from start to finish","It includes every element contained in the written note","It avoids all medical terminology in favour of plain language","It leads the listener to the same differential the presenter formulated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a functional test rather than a stylistic one, and the sharpest single criterion given. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 9"},{"q":"Which components of the history are taken in focused rather than comprehensive form during a focused encounter?","choices":["None — a focused encounter narrows the physical examination but never narrows the history at all","History of present illness, review of systems, past medical history and social history","Only the history of present illness, with everything else taken in full","Only the review of systems and the family history"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — all four are narrowed, alongside family history, medications and allergies. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 7"},{"q":"Which findings belong in an oral case presentation?","choices":["Pertinent positives and negatives from both the history and the physical examination","Every finding obtained, whether positive or negative","Only the abnormal findings, since normal findings add nothing","Only the physical examination findings, since the history is documented separately"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Choosing what counts as pertinent is the clinical reasoning being assessed. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 9"},{"q":"What must be done if part of the examination was not performed?","choices":["Obtain the finding from a classmate who examined the same patient","Document why it was not done — findings may never be invented","Record the finding that would be expected if it were normal","Leave the section blank without further comment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recording something you did not do is the one error the guidance treats as absolute. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 20"},{"q":"What advice is given about using notes while presenting a case?","choices":["Notes are not permitted during a case presentation at all","Hand the notes to the listener rather than presenting aloud","Try not to read from them","Read from them verbatim so that no detail is omitted"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — reading works directly against helping the listener visualise the patient. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 9"},{"q":"Beyond taking a history and examining the patient, what else must be produced in a focused clinical encounter?","choices":["A written history and physical document completed afterwards","A referral letter to the appropriate specialist","Differentials, laboratory and imaging studies, a diagnosis, and a treatment plan including patient education","A diagnosis and a prescription, with the reasoning behind them left implicit rather than stated aloud"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The encounter tests the whole reasoning chain rather than data gathering alone. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 7"},{"q":"In what order should an oral case presentation generally proceed?","choices":["Mostly the order in which the history was obtained and the examination performed","In reverse, opening with the diagnosis and working back to the evidence","Grouped by body system in alphabetical order","Physical examination findings first, then the history that prompted them"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Following the encounter's own sequence is what makes it easy to follow. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 9"},{"q":"When a third party supplies the patient's responses during an assessed encounter, where should your attention stay?","choices":["On whoever is supplying the answers, since that is the source of information","On the documentation form, so that responses are recorded accurately","Divided evenly between the patient and the person answering","On the patient, interacting with the patient rather than the person supplying the answers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is stated emphatically. The encounter is with the patient even when the words come from elsewhere. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 8"},{"q":"How should physical examination findings be recorded in a written clinical note?","choices":["Record only the abnormal findings and omit the normal ones entirely","Assign each system a numeric score on a standard scale","Describe what was found, rather than writing normal, abnormal or unremarkable","Use unremarkable wherever nothing is wrong, to keep the note concise"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a description communicates what was actually observed, where a label does not. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 20"},{"q":"Why can reusing your own earlier written work create an academic integrity problem?","choices":["Reused documents often carry formatting errors","Faculty cannot grade the same document twice","Work is not original when it has already been submitted for academic credit, whether by you or anyone else","Earlier work may contain clinical guidance that has since changed and would now be inaccurate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which is why reusing a template from a previous assignment carries a warning. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 22"},{"q":"What should be reviewed before submitting a written clinical assignment?","choices":["Only the textbook chapter on documentation","The grading rubric, and the comments left on previous assignments","A classmate's completed version of the very same assignment for comparison","The academic calendar and the course schedule"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — prior feedback is the most direct guide to what still needs improving. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 20"},{"q":"How should a student handle feedback that differs between facilitators?","choices":["Follow only the most senior facilitator's feedback","Ask the course team for a single standardised rubric so that any disagreement between facilitators is settled","Disregard feedback that conflicts with earlier guidance","Accept the constructive feedback and modify behaviour, recognising that different facilitators give different feedback"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the variation is anticipated rather than treated as a contradiction to be resolved. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 23"}]};
